# Chapter 9, third part: the command words of the math notation.

.h2 Greek letters [eq-greek]
.ix Greek letters
The Greek letters are commands made of the English name of the letter. A name
that begins with a small letter gives the small letter and a name that begins
with a capital gives the capital. Where a capital Greek letter looks like a
Latin capital (Alpha, Beta, Epsilon, Zeta, Eta, Iota, Kappa, Mu, Nu, Omicron,
Rho, Tau, Chi) TeX has no command for it: type the Latin letter, which is set
upright, as capitals of math are. A small Greek letter is set in the italic of
math.  The names that begin with var give the second form of the letter that
some fonts have; the names that are marked amssymb are there because the
equation is typeset with the package amssymb.

