The sign < is the less than sign and > the greater than sign, typed as they
are.  Do not use the angle brackets of the keyboard for the brackets of a
vector: use {c:\langle} and {c:\rangle} ({n:eq-delimiters}).

.h2 Arrows [eq-arrows]
.ix Arrows
The arrows are relations, so they have a thick space on both sides.  The names
say the direction: left, right, up, down, and the long forms, whose names
begin with long, are longer arrows. A name that begins with a capital
(Rightarrow) is the double arrow. {c:\implies}, {c:\impliedby} and {c:\iff}
are the long double arrows with more space round them, as used between
statements.  Arrows that stretch over a label are in {x:eq-accents}
({c:\xrightarrow}).

