{c:\jmath} are for putting an accent on them ({n:eq-accents}).

.h2 Superscripts and subscripts [eq-scripts]
.ix Superscripts and subscripts
The sign ^ raises what follows it to the superscript place and the sign _
lowers it to the subscript place.  What is taken is the next character or the
next group; to raise or lower more than one character, use braces.  Both signs
can follow one thing, in either order.  The scripts of a script are smaller
again, to three levels.  Scripts that stand before a thing are made by putting
an empty group in front.  The results of this table are set in display size, as
an equation that stands alone: the limits of a sum are above and below it.  In
a line of text the same limits go at the side; the commands {c:\limits} and
{c:\nolimits} change that for one operator.

In the tables of this group the result is typeset with {c:\displaystyle} in
front of the source, which is not shown, so that it has the form of an
equation that stands alone.

.table 25 15 18
Source | Result | Meaning
{c:x^2} | {eq:\displaystyle x^2} | superscript of one character
{c:x_i} | {eq:\displaystyle x_i} | subscript of one character
{c:x_i^2} | {eq:\displaystyle x_i^2} | both (either order)
{c:x^{{n+1}}} | {eq:\displaystyle x^{{n+1}}} | a group as superscript
{c:x_{{ij}}} | {eq:\displaystyle x_{{ij}}} | a group as subscript
{c:x^{{y^z}}} | {eq:\displaystyle x^{{y^z}}} | superscript of a superscript
{c:a_{{i_j}}} | {eq:\displaystyle a_{{i_j}}} | subscript of a subscript
{c:x^{{a}}_{{b}}} | {eq:\displaystyle x^{{a}}_{{b}}} | both with groups
{c:x^2_3} | {eq:\displaystyle x^2_3} | each script takes one character without
 braces
{c:x'} | {eq:\displaystyle x'} | prime (an apostrophe is a prime)
{c:x''} | {eq:\displaystyle x''} | double prime
{c:f'(x)} | {eq:\displaystyle f'(x)} | derivative
{c:x^\prime} | {eq:\displaystyle x^\prime} | prime as a command
{c:{{}}^{{14}}_{{6}}\mathrm{{C}}} | {eq:\displaystyle
 {{}}^{{14}}_{{6}}\mathrm{{C}}} | scripts on the left (an empty group first)
{c:e^{{-x^2/2}}} | {eq:\displaystyle e^{{-x^2/2}}} | scripts that hold an
 expression
{c:\sum_{{i=1}}^{{n}}} | {eq:\displaystyle \sum_{{i=1}}^{{n}}} | limits of a
 sum
{c:\sum\nolimits_{{i=1}}^{{n}}} | {eq:\displaystyle
 \sum\nolimits_{{i=1}}^{{n}}} | limits at the side
{c:\int\limits_0^1} | {eq:\displaystyle \int\limits_0^1} | limits over and
 under an integral
{c:\lim_{{x\to 0}}} | {eq:\displaystyle \lim_{{x\to 0}}} | limit under the
 word lim
{c:\max_{{1\le i\le n}}} | {eq:\displaystyle \max_{{1\le i\le n}}} | limit
 under max
{c:\overset{{!}}{{=}}} | {eq:\displaystyle \overset{{!}}{{=}}} | a mark over a
 symbol
{c:\underset{{n\to\infty}}{{\lim}}} | {eq:\displaystyle
 \underset{{n\to\infty}}{{\lim}}} | text under a symbol
{c:\sideset{{_a^b}}{{_c^d}}\sum} | {eq:\displaystyle
 \sideset{{_a^b}}{{_c^d}}\sum} | scripts on both sides of an operator
.end

The apostrophe is a prime and stands for the superscript {c:^\prime}; a second
apostrophe makes a double prime. A script on something that is already
scripted (x^2^3) is an error of TeX, with the message "Double superscript";
put one of the two in a group.

.h2 Fractions, binomials and roots [eq-fractions]
.ix Fractions, binomials and roots
{c:\frac} takes two arguments, the numerator and the denominator, and
{c:\sqrt} one, the radicand; with the square brackets {c:[n]} before the
argument it takes the n-th root.  The variants {c:\dfrac} and {c:\tfrac} force
the display and the text size of the fraction, and {c:\cfrac} sets a continued
fraction with full sized parts. {c:\binom} is the binomial coefficient: two
arguments, no bar, between large parentheses.  The TeX forms {c:{{a \over
b}}} and {c:{{n \choose k}}} also work: the command takes everything in the
group, to the left and to the right.  The results are set in display size.

The tables of this group typeset the result as {x:eq-scripts} says.

.table 25 15 18
Source | Result | Meaning
