In a line of text a fraction is small: its parts are set in the size of a
script.  In an equation that stands alone the fraction is large.  Begin an
inline equation with {c:\displaystyle} to get the large fraction in a line of
text.

.h2 Large operators and functions [eq-bigops]
.ix Large operators and functions
The large operators (sum, product, integral, union ...) are set larger in a
displayed equation and have their limits above and below; in an inline
equation they are small and the limits are at the side.  The limits are typed
as a subscript and a superscript.  The names of the usual functions are
commands: they set the name upright and put the right space round it, and the
ones that have limits (lim, max, min, sup, inf, det, gcd, Pr and others) take
their subscript underneath in a displayed equation.  The results of this table
are set in display size.

The tables of this group typeset the result as {x:eq-scripts} says.

