For a function that has no command, use {c:\operatorname{{name}}} (or
{c:\operatorname*{{name}}} when it has limits underneath).  Do not type the
letters of the name as they are: they would be set as variables ({eq:sin x}
against {eq:\sin x}).  The package declaration that other documents use for it
({c:\DeclareMathOperator}) is refused in an equation ({n:eq-refused}).

.h2 Delimiters and their sizes [eq-delimiters]
.ix Delimiters and their sizes
A delimiter is a bracket, a bar or a similar sign.  The keyboard brackets are
delimiters as they are.  The braces, which have a meaning of their own in the
text, are typed {c:\{{} and {c:\}}}. {c:\left} and {c:\right} in front of a
delimiter make a pair that is as large as what is between them; every
{c:\left} needs its {c:\right} in the same group, and a dot ({c:\left.}) makes
one side empty. {c:\big}, {c:\Big}, {c:\bigg} and {c:\Bigg} give four fixed
larger sizes, and {c:\bigl} and {c:\bigr} (and the other l and r forms) tell
the side so that the spacing is that of an opening and of a closing sign.
{c:\middle} puts a delimiter between the other two that grows with them.

.table 25 15 18
Source | Result | Meaning
{c:( x )} | {eq:( x )} | parentheses
{c:[ x ]} | {eq:[ x ]} | square brackets
{c:\{{ x \}}} | {eq:\{{ x \}}} | braces (backslash needed)
{c:\langle x \rangle} | {eq:\langle x \rangle} | angle brackets
{c:\lvert x \rvert} | {eq:\lvert x \rvert} | absolute value bars
{c:\lVert x \rVert} | {eq:\lVert x \rVert} | norm bars
{c:\lfloor x \rfloor} | {eq:\lfloor x \rfloor} | floor
{c:\lceil x \rceil} | {eq:\lceil x \rceil} | ceiling
{c:| x |} | {eq:| x |} | vertical bars typed as they are
{c:\| x \|} | {eq:\| x \|} | double bars
{c:\vert x \vert} | {eq:\vert x \vert} | vertical bars by name
{c:\Vert x \Vert} | {eq:\Vert x \Vert} | double bars by name
{c:\ulcorner x \urcorner} | {eq:\ulcorner x \urcorner} | upper corners
 (amssymb)
{c:\big( x \big)} | {eq:\big( x \big)} | one size larger
{c:\Big( x \Big)} | {eq:\Big( x \Big)} | two sizes larger
{c:\bigg( x \bigg)} | {eq:\bigg( x \bigg)} | three sizes larger
{c:\Bigg( x \Bigg)} | {eq:\Bigg( x \Bigg)} | four sizes larger
{c:\bigl( x \bigr)} | {eq:\bigl( x \bigr)} | same as \big with the side told
{c:\left( \frac{{a}}{{b}} \right)} | {eq:\left( \frac{{a}}{{b}} \right)} |
