.end

On an i or a j use {c:\imath} or {c:\jmath}, so that the dot does not collide
with the accent.  The accents of text ({c:\'e} and the others) and letters with
accents that are typed on the keyboard cannot be used in math: TeX stops with
the message "Please use {c:\mathaccent} for accents in math mode" (and the
characters above 127 are rejected by the printing program in any case).  For
accented
words in a formula put them in {c:\text}.

.h2 Text and fonts [eq-fonts]
.ix Text and fonts
Letters in a formula are variables and are set in the math italic.  The font
commands change that for their argument. {c:\text} sets its argument as text,
in the font of the text round the equation and with real spaces; it is the
command for words in a formula.  The font commands for math change the letters,
digits and (for {c:\boldsymbol}) the symbols, but do not make words with
spaces.  The size commands {c:\displaystyle}, {c:\textstyle}, {c:\scriptstyle}
and {c:\scriptscriptstyle} choose the style of what follows them in the group.

.table 25 15 18
